  "response": "Having already inferred the existence and position of Neptune from irregularities in the motion of Uranus, Le Verrier was led by that earlier success to attribute the Mercury anomaly to unseen matter orbiting between Mercury and the Sun. That attribution led directly to a baptism: the hypothetical intra-Mercurial planet was given the name Vulcan. A body bearing that name could be sought only in the rare circumstances that suppress the solar glare, namely during a passage across the face of the Sun or in the few minutes of a total eclipse.\n\nThe observational record opens with a report from a provincial French physician. Edmond Lescarbault reported seeing a dark object transit the solar disk in 1859. Only afterwards did the matter acquire institutional standing: Le Verrier confirmed Vulcan telescopically in 1860 and published its orbital elements, so that the celestial mechanics of the supposed body followed the amateur sighting rather than preceding it.\n\nObservers at the 1878 total solar eclipse in North America reported starlike objects close to the Sun. Later analysis of the 1878 eclipse observations determined, subsequently to those reports and on the basis of careful positional reduction, that the reported objects were already-catalogued stars. That identification of the eclipse objects with known stars shows that repeated eclipse and transit searches produced no reproducible detection of an intra-Mercurial body.\n\nThe question was ultimately settled by recognising that the situation must be one of two mutually exclusive ones, and only one. Either no intra-Mercurial planet has ever been confirmed to exist, or a planet named Vulcan orbits the Sun inside the orbit of Mercury; exactly one of these two states of affairs holds, and they cannot hold together. The second of them, a Vulcan circling the Sun within Mercury's orbit, cannot be squared with the finding that the value predicted by general relativity agreed with the observed residual without invoking any additional matter, since a relativistic account that needs no extra mass leaves no gravitational work for such a planet to do.\n\nThe problem took its modern form in the middle of the nineteenth century, when Urbain Le Verrier announced in 1859 that Mercury's perihelion advanced faster than Newtonian theory predicted. Once the gravitational tugs of the other known planets have been computed and subtracted from the observed motion, the unexplained portion of Mercury's perihelion advance amounts to about 43 arcseconds per century. The portion of Mercury's perihelion precession left unexplained by planetary perturbations is 574 arcseconds per century. A shortfall of some 43 arcseconds per century and a shortfall of 574 arcseconds per century cannot both be what the perturbation calculation fails to account for; accepting either figure as the residual excludes the other.",
